<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Developing trustworthy intelligent vision systems for high-stakes domains, <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, remote sensing and medical diagnosis, demands broad robustness without costly retraining. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual Reasoning Agent (VRA)</span>, a training-free, agentic reasoning framework that wraps off-the-shelf vision-language models <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> pure vision systems in a <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Think-Critique-Act</em> loop. While VRA incurs significant additional test-time computation, it achieves up to 40% absolute accuracy gains on challenging visual reasoning benchmarks. Future work will optimize query routing and early stopping to reduce inference overhead while preserving reliability in vision tasks.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">K</em><span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">eywords</span> Agentic AI, Reasoning AI, Vision-Language Model, AI Robustness, Test-time Compute</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Artificial intelligence (AI) enabled vision systems-including pure vision models and large vision-language models (LVLMs)-often fail to generalize reliably across high-stakes tasks such as remote sensing and medical diagnosis. Fine-tuning can be prohibitively expensive, demands extensive labeled data, and offers no robustness guarantees, while many teams lack retraining resources. A training-free, modular framework that leverages existing off-the-shelf models can immediately bolster task reliability without extra data or parameter updates.
Current LVLMs often struggle with complex visual reasoning and intricate perceptual tasks requiring deep understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Prior research on enhancing LVLM factuality through post-hoc correction methods (e.g., Visual Fact Checker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, LogicCheckGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>) typically employs rigid, predefined pipelines. These methods are limited in adapting to varied visual tasks demanding complex reasoning, as they lack autonomous, dynamic decision-making.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic AI offers a promising alternative. Recent advances in Large Language Model (LLM)-based agentic reasoning (e.g., ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, CRITIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>) show potential for self-correcting AI agents that iteratively refine outputs via multi-step reasoning and external tool integration, leading to improved robustness. While explored in text-based tasks, agentic AI enhancing general robustness across diverse vision tasks in LVLMs remains underexplored <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We posit that robustifying end-to-end vision tasks-such as remote sensing and medical diagnosis-poses unique challenges: fine-tuning requires large, domain-specific labeled datasets with no guarantee of improved resilience, and many teams lack the resources or expertise for model retraining. In contrast, a training-free, modular framework can be deployed immediately using off-the-shelf commercial or open-source vision-language models, enhancing reliability on the final task without data collection or tuning costs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we introduce the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual Reasoning Agent (VRA)</span>, an agentic framework that wraps plug-in LVLMs in an adaptive <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Think-Critique-Act</em> loop. VRA orchestrates multiple vision-language models in a modular pipeline-leveraging cross-model verification and iterative self-correction over visual inputs-to trade additional test-time computation for substantial robustness gains. By orchestrating cross-model reasoning and self-critique at inference time, VRA further enables responsible and reliable AI deployment, providing transparent, auditable decision flows rather than opaque single-model outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are: a novel <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Think–Critique–Act</em> agentic loop that dynamically coordinates multiple plug-in LVLMs and vision models for enhanced visual reasoning, operating effectively with minimal prompts and enabling transparent, auditable inference flows for responsible AI deployment; empirical validation on the VRSBench VQA dataset showing up to 40% absolute accuracy improvements across challenging remote sensing benchmarks, confirming that inference-time reasoning markedly boosts reliability; and a training-free, composable design deployable via existing commercial APIs or local open-source models, lowering the barrier to entry and laying the groundwork for responsible, reliable vision deployments in high-stakes domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Proposed Agent Framework</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the VRA framework to enhance the general robustness of LVLMs across diverse visual tasks (perception and reasoning). VRA integrates a <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Think-Critique-Act</em> loop, inspired by the ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and Reflexion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, for iterative refinement and self-correction. The VRA framework operates through specialized LLM-based agents, each contributing to a dynamic, iterative reasoning process. The agent maintains a shared memory space for retrieved information and critiques. The workflow as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Proposed Agent Framework ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is structured as follows:
<span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Captioner:</span> An initial LVLM generates a preliminary image caption, providing foundational visual context.
<span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drafter:</span> An LLM formulates an initial answer to the user’s query based on the caption. The drafter’s response includes this initial answer, a self-critique, and a proposed follow-up question intended for a visual AI model.
<span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inquirer:</span> An LLM takes the question generated by the drafter and queries one or multiple LVLMs for further visual information.
<span id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vision-Language Suite:</span> One or multiple vision models answer the same query, providing diverse visual information. This multi-model approach is central to VRA’s robustness through cross-verification and reduced reliance on a single model.
<span id="S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Revisor:</span> The revisor, an LLM, refines the previous answer by incorporating the new visual information. Similar to the drafter, its response includes a revised answer, an updated self-critique, and another question for further verification. This forms a core part of the iterative refinement.
<span id="S2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spokesman:</span> An LLM determines the final answer, integrating insights from the last revisor’s conversation history.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We posit that increasing test-time compute through such iterative agentic reasoning significantly enhances robustness. Instead of relying on a single inference pass, the agent’s ability to “think", “critique", then “act" by querying multiple models and refining its understanding over several steps leads to more accurate and reliable outputs. This is particularly beneficial for complex visual reasoning tasks where a superficial understanding is insufficient. The computational cost, while higher, is justifiable in high-stakes domains like medical imaging, where accuracy and robustness outweigh throughput. Note that we set three iterations for the loop. The agents’ prompt templates are in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompt Templates ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16343v1/img/vra_workflow.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/168;" width="429" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The architecture and workflow of our proposed framework, VRA.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminary Experiments and Results</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our hypothesis, we conducted preliminary experiments on visual reasoning tasks within the remote sensing (RS) domain, a high-stakes application area where is critical for applications like disaster response.
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset.</span>
We evaluated VRA on the VRSBench VQA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which offers comprehensive satellite imagery understanding tasks (object-level perception and complex visual reasoning). We sampled 50 questions per question type (ten total types). Performance is measured by accuracy per question type and overall accuracy is computed as the average across all evaluated question types.
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span>
To demonstrate VRA’s adaptability and robustness, we integrated it with multiple established LVLMs as plug-in components. These baseline LVLMs include: GeoChat (7B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, LLaVA-v1.5 (7B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and Gemma 3 (12B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Note that GeoChat is a specialized RS-LVLM, fine-tuned on LLaVA-v1.5 using RS datasets. The core agent backbone is QwQ (32B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, a reasoning model solely operating on textual inputs. Also, Phi-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is used in VRSBench VQA dataset for evaluating free-form answers.
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VRA Variants.</span>
VRA is designed to be modular, allowing for the integration of single or multiple plug-in LVLMs as visual information providers. We define the following VRA variants for comparison: <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VRA (LVLM-Name)</span>, which denotes VRA with only the specified LVLM (e.g., GeoChat, LLaVA-1.5, Gemma 3) as the visual information source. In Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Results Analysis ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Results Analysis ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VRA (+1 LVLM avg)</span> represents the average of VRA (GeoChat), VRA (LLaVA-1.5), and VRA (Gemma 3). Also, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LVLM alone (avg)</span> in the figures refers to the average performance of the three baseline LVLMs (GeoChat, LLaVA-1.5, and Gemma 3) without VRA. <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VRA (+2 LVLMs)</span> utilizes GeoChat and LLaVA-1.5 within URA. Lastly, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VRA (+3 LVLMs)</span> integrates all three baseline LVLMs (GeoChat, LLaVA-1.5, and Gemma 3) concurrently.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Accuracy Improvement.</span>
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Results Analysis ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, VRA consistently and significantly improves the overall accuracy of various plug-in LVLMs. For instance, GeoChat’s standalone overall accuracy of 46.40% increased to 66.80% (20.40% improvement) when integrated with VRA. Similarly, LLaVA-1.5 saw a 14.20% increase (57.60% to 71.80%), and Gemma 3 rose by 15.60% (54.40% to 70.00%). These results indicate substantial enhancement in both factual and reasoning-intensive VQA tasks when augmented with our agentic reasoning framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Enhanced Performance Across Question Types.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Results Analysis ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows VRA variants consistently outperform the LVLM alone (avg) baseline across nearly all ten question types. This confirms our agentic approach significantly improves overall performance on challenging perceptual and reasoning tasks. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Results Analysis ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that in “object quantity" and “object direction" question types, VRA demonstrated accuracy improvements of up to 40% compared to the baseline average. Further detailed numerical results are in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Comprehensive Results Details ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime Analysis</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Results Analysis ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the trade-off between increased robustness and computational cost. While standalone baseline LVLMs average 1.52 minutes per task, incorporating our agentic framework substantially increases this duration. <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VRA (+1 LVLM avg)</span> averages 155.44 minutes, increasing to 155.59 minutes for <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VRA (+2 LVLMs)</span> and 189.09 minutes for <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VRA (+3 LVLMs)</span>. This demonstrates that robustness gains from extensive multi-model interactions and iterative reasoning, demanding higher test-time compute, come at the cost of increased inference time.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16343v1/img/plot-acc.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/109;" width="238" height="109" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overall accuracy comparison between standalone LVLMs and VRA-augmented variants, showing consistent performance improvement.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16343v1/img/plot-qtype_acc.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/225;" width="238" height="225" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Per-question-type accuracy of averaged baseline LVLM versus VRA configurations, demonstrating enhanced robustness across most question types. Refer to Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Experiment Setup ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> for the definitions of VRA variants.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16343v1/img/plot-runtime.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/138;" width="238" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Overall runtime comparison between averaged baseline LVLMs and VRA configurations, highlighting increased runtime with higher test-time compute. Refer to Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Experiment Setup ‣ 3 Preliminary Experiments and Results ‣ Agentic Reasoning for Robust Vision Systems via Increased Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> for the definitions of VRA variants.</figcaption>
</figure>
