</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our preliminary findings from VRA provide compelling evidence that agentic reasoning significantly enhances the general robustness of LVLMs across diverse visual tasks. The substantial accuracy improvements in remote sensing for both perceptual and reasoning tasks, coupled with VRA’s architectural components, underscore the potential for more reliable and trustworthy LVLM deployments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Iterative Agentic Reasoning and Diverse Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Think–Critique–Act</em> Loop allows VRA to dynamically refine its understanding and outputs. Unlike static inference, the agent’s ability to recursively draft, inquire, and revise enables it to navigate complex visual information and resolve inconsistencies. This dynamic adaptation is crucial for high accuracy across visual reasoning challenges, as evidenced by VRA’s consistent outperformance of baseline LVLMs. The robustness gained is from iterative self-correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By leveraging a vision-language suite with multiple plug-in LVLMs, VRA inherently mitigates risks associated with reliance on a single model. Each integrated LVLM offers a distinct visual encoder and training bias, enabling cross-model verification. This multi-expert approach detects and addresses inconsistencies, enhancing factual consistency and reducing susceptibility to individual model failures. Our experiments showed robust performance gains even when LVLMs share features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The agent backbone (e.g., QwQ) plays a pivotal role by contributing its intrinsic reasoning capabilities. VRA leverages the agent’s powerful “thinking" to formulate intelligent follow-up questions, critique responses, and synthesize information from disparate sources. This enables effective visual reasoning even with basic initial prompts, highlighting that robustness stems from the agentic workflow, the underlying reasoning model, and effective orchestration of diverse vision model interactions, also contributing to make the agent’s decision-making process more transparent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Responsibility and Reliability</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our results show that VRA improves various LVLMs up to 40% across multiple complex visual tasks, which provides a strong indicator of reliability and generalizability. Also, the agentic reasoning inherently provides auditable process, which paves the path toward responsible AI. Moreover, VRA’s multi-expert nature, where information is cross-verified, potentially provides an inherent defense against adversarial attacks. Other than malicious perturbations, the intrinsic model errors, so-called hallucinations might be able to be mitigated by systematically evaluating consistency and factual accuracy across multiple visual information sources in VRA. This indicates the potential of VRA in a broader spectrum of robustness. However, further rigorous evaluation of both adversarial robustness and hallucination mitigation is required.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Limitations and Future Directions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our VRA framework is not without limitations, primarily related to computational overhead. As shown in runtime analysis, leveraging a long-thinking process reasoning model and increasing the number of integrated LVLMs leads to a substantial increase in inference time, which could be a barrier for real-time applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Future work will focus on:
(1) Investigating more efficient agentic frameworks that retain robustness while significantly reducing computational overhead (e.g., intelligent query routing, early stopping when VRA reaches high confidence).
(2) Evaluating VRA on hallucination benchmarks (e.g., Throne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>) for intrinsic errors assurance.
(3) Rigorously validating VRA’s adversarial robustness against a wider array of state-of-the-art attacks, exploring adaptive, training-free defense mechanisms.
(4) Further testing on diverse visual domains (e.g., medical imaging) to assess VRA’s generalizability for real-world reliability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Robust vision systems-whether pure vision models or vision-language models-are essential for high-stakes tasks such as remote sensing and medical diagnosis. We have introduced <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VRA</span>, a training-free, agentic reasoning framework that wraps off-the-shelf vision models and LVLMs in a <em id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Think-Critique-Act</em> loop. Empirical results on remote sensing benchmarks demonstrate up to 40% absolute accuracy gains, confirming that increased test-time compute via agentic reasoning is a justifiable trade-off for reliability.
VRA’s modular, training-free design democratizes access to robust vision deployments, even for teams lacking fine-tuning resources. Moreover, its multi-expert verification and iterative self-correction principles point toward promising defenses against adversarial perturbations and hallucinations. Future work will focus on optimizing query routing and early stopping to reduce inference overhead and extending validation to medical imaging and other critical vision domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
